{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-70819",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨1,-2,3⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y+3z & =0 \\\\ 3z & =-x+2y \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{2}{3}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=0.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,0)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,0⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨1,-2,3⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
